          Most driveway sealer labels say the same thing in small print: 50°F and rising, for application and for the following 24 hours. That line is a floor, not a target. A coat that goes down at 52°F on a gray afternoon and drops to 44°F overnight can look fine at dinner and peel by spring. This guide breaks the decision into three readings you can check with a phone and a cheap infrared thermometer, and explains what each one is protecting.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="three-numbers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Three Numbers That Decide the Day
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you open a pail, check three temperatures. The first is <strong>air temperature during application</strong>, which most asphalt emulsion and coal tar labels set at a minimum of 50°F, rising. The second is <strong>pavement temperature</strong>, which can run 20 to 50 degrees above or below the air depending on sun, shade and the hour. The third is the <strong>low over the next 24 hours</strong>, because the film keeps curing through the night and stalls if the slab gets cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            If all three sit comfortably inside the label range, the rest of the job is technique. If any one of them is marginal, the coat is at risk no matter how carefully you squeegee it. The practical target for water-based sealers is an air temperature of roughly 60 to 85°F, pavement between 60 and about 120°F, and an overnight low that stays at or above 50°F. That is tighter than the legal minimum on the label, and that margin is the point.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why aim above the minimum? Because forecasts miss by a few degrees all the time, and a sealer that needed 50°F does not care that the app said 52°F. Building in five to ten degrees of cushion means a slightly cooler night or a cloud bank in the afternoon does not push the film out of its working range. The cost of waiting one more week for a warmer stretch is nothing compared with the cost of stripping and redoing a failed coat.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Reading</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label minimum (typical)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Comfortable target</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Walk away if</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Air during application</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50°F and rising</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-85°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 50°F or over 90°F</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pavement surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-120°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 50°F or over about 130°F</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Low in next 24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">55°F or warmer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Forecast at or below 45°F</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rain-free window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies, often 24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Showers inside 8 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those ranges apply to the water-based asphalt emulsion, coal tar emulsion and acrylic products that make up almost everything sold at home centers. Always defer to the specific label on your pail when it is stricter. Some premium products ask for 55°F, and a few fast-dry formulas list a slightly wider band. Our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">ranked list of asphalt driveway sealers</a> notes the application window for each pick so you can compare before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-cold-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Cold Pavement Ruins a Coat That Looks Fine
          </h2>

          <p>
            A water-based driveway sealer is a suspension: microscopic particles of asphalt or refined coal tar, plus clay, fillers and sometimes sand, held in water by an emulsifier. When you spread it, the water starts to leave by evaporation and by soaking slightly into the pavement. As it goes, the particles crowd together and fuse into a continuous film. Paint chemists call that step coalescence, and it is the step that cold weather breaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fusion needs the binder to be soft enough to flow into its neighbors. Below roughly 50°F, asphalt particles are too stiff to merge fully. The water still leaves, more slowly, and the surface turns the right shade of black. Underneath that color you can end up with a layer of particles that are touching but not truly bonded. It feels dry, it looks dry, and it has the strength of chalk.
          </p>

          <p>
            The failure tends to show up weeks later. Common signs owners report after a cold-weather job include gray streaks where tires turn, powder that rubs off on a shoe, and patches that lift in sheets after the first hard freeze or the first pass of a snow shovel. None of those can be fixed with a touch-up coat, because the new layer is bonding to a weak one. The usual fix is a stiff-bristle scrub, a rinse, and a recoat once proper weather returns.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Dry to the touch is not cured.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A coat can pass the fingertip test in two hours on a cool day and still need days of warm weather to reach full strength. Judge readiness by the temperatures it will see over the next 24 hours, not by how it looks at sundown.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Cold also slows evaporation, which stretches the period when the film is vulnerable to dew and rain. A coat that would be rain-safe in four hours at 80°F may need twelve or more at 55°F. If you are weighing a borderline day, read our guide to <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">what rain does to fresh sealer</a> before you commit, because cool and damp together are the worst pairing there is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pavement-temp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Pavement Temperature Is the Reading Most People Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Asphalt is a dark mass that soaks up sunlight and releases it slowly. On a clear July afternoon with air at 85°F, a black driveway in full sun can read 130 to 150°F. On a cool, clear morning after a cold night, the same slab can sit well below air temperature until the sun has been on it for a couple of hours. The weather app has no idea which of those you are standing on.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inexpensive infrared thermometer, the kind sold for checking ovens or tires for roughly $20 to $40 at the time of writing, settles the question in seconds. Take readings in the sunniest spot, the shadiest spot, and the area under any tree or along the north side of the house. The coldest reading is the one that counts for the minimum, and the hottest is the one that counts for the maximum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shade creates the most common split. A driveway that runs alongside a garage may have a strip that never gets direct sun in October, sitting ten or fifteen degrees colder than the open section. If that strip reads below 50°F, either seal the sunny part now and come back to the shaded part on a warmer day, or wait until the whole slab is in range. Sealing it anyway because the rest of the drive is fine is how people end up with a perfect driveway and one gray stripe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Morning timing matters for the same reason. A slab that sat at 42°F at dawn is still cold at 9 a.m. even if the air has reached 58°F. Late morning to early afternoon is usually the sweet spot in spring and fall, after the pavement has warmed and well before evening cooling starts. In summer, the logic reverses, which the hot-weather section below covers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Pavement Readings</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">130-150°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Black asphalt, full July sun</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10-15°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Shade gap vs. sunny section</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sun needed to warm a cold slab</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$20-$40</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Infrared thermometer cost</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="overnight-low" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Overnight Low Matters as Much as the Afternoon High
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 24-hour clause on most labels exists because curing does not stop when you put the squeegee away. The film continues to shed water and tighten up through the evening and night. If the pavement drops below about 50°F during that time, fusion stalls partway. The coat you laid in perfect afternoon sun can end up with the same weak structure as one applied in the cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the hourly forecast, not just the daily low. A night that bottoms out at 51°F at 6 a.m. is very different from one that hits 46°F by midnight and stays there. Also note that low-lying driveways, ones near open fields, and drives at the bottom of a slope can run several degrees colder than the official station reading on clear, calm nights, when cold air pools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clouds help. A cloudy night holds heat in the pavement, while a clear, still night lets it radiate away fast. If the forecast shows a clear sky and a low within five degrees of your label minimum, treat it as a fail. If it shows cloud cover and a low a few degrees above the minimum, you have more margin than the number suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the job so the coat gets the warmest part of the day to set up. Finishing by early afternoon gives the film four to six hours of the day's peak heat before evening. Starting a full driveway at 4 p.m. in late September, even on a warm day, hands most of the cure to the coldest hours. If you only have evenings free, a weekend in late spring or midsummer is a better bet than an autumn weeknight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hot-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When It Is Too Hot to Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat causes a different failure. When the pavement is extremely hot, the water flashes off before the sealer can level out. The result is lap marks where each squeegee pass overlaps, a rough, streaky texture, and in bad cases fine surface cracking as the film skins over and then shrinks. Many labels cap application at around 90°F air temperature, and some contractors stop well before that on bright days.
          </p>

          <p>
            The standard fix on a hot day is to <strong>mist the pavement</strong> with a garden hose just before you spread, leaving it damp but with no standing water. That cools the surface and slows the first stage of drying so the sealer has time to flow. Several manufacturers recommend exactly this for hot conditions; check that your label allows it, then work in smaller sections so each one is sealed while the surface is still damp.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing beats any trick. In midsummer, start early in the morning, before the slab has absorbed much sun, and aim to finish by late morning. The coat then cures through a warm afternoon, which is ideal, without having been spread on a surface hot enough to fry it. Evening application in summer can also work if the night stays above 60°F, though dew becomes a risk in humid regions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not thin the sealer with extra water to make it spread easier in the heat unless the label permits a specific amount. Over-diluting reduces the solids that form the film and weakens the coat regardless of temperature. If the product is too thick to work on a hot day, that is a sign to wait for a milder morning, not to add water from the hose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="humidity-sun" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Humidity, Dew, Shade and Wind
          </h2>

          <p>
            Temperature sets the boundaries, but humidity sets the pace. Water-based sealers dry by evaporation, so a 60°F day at 40 percent humidity can cure a coat faster than a 70°F day at 90 percent. On muggy days, expect the dry times on the label to stretch by half or more, and plan traffic and rain margins accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dew is the hidden risk on fall evenings. When the pavement cools below the dew point, moisture condenses on it just as it does on a car windshield. On a coat that has not yet set, dew can leave a dull, blotchy finish or a gray haze. If the evening forecast shows the temperature dropping close to the dew point, seal early enough that the film has several hours of drying before dusk.
          </p>

          <p>
            A light breeze helps by carrying moisture away from the surface. Strong, gusty wind does the opposite of what you want on a hot day, skinning the top of the film over before the layer below has dried, and it blows leaves, grass clippings and dust into wet sealer. A steady 5 to 10 mph is close to ideal. Gusts above about 20 mph are a reason to wait.
          </p>

          <p>
            Direct sun is generally a friend in spring and fall and a challenge in summer. A driveway with mixed sun and shade will cure unevenly, so expect the shaded sections to stay tacky longer, and keep cars off the whole drive until the slowest section is ready. For a step-by-step on sequencing a mixed-exposure drive, our <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">driveway sealing walkthrough</a> covers where to start and how to work toward the exit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-sealer-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How the Window Changes by Sealer Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Asphalt emulsion</strong> is the most common DIY product and the most temperature sensitive of the water-based group. The usual rule of 50°F and rising for 24 hours applies, and it is happiest in the 60 to 85°F range. Because the binder is softer than coal tar, it can tolerate a little heat, but it is also the most likely to powder if it cures cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Refined coal tar emulsion</strong> follows similar temperature rules. Its sale and use are now banned or restricted in a growing number of states and cities because of concerns about polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, or PAHs, in runoff and dust, so check local rules before buying. Where it is allowed, wear gloves and eye protection and keep it off skin, because it is a known irritant.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Acrylic and polymer-modified sealers</strong> tend to have similar minimums but cure into a tougher film, and some list a slightly faster rain-safe time. They are usually the priciest option per gallon. <strong>Solvent-based or oil-based penetrating sealers</strong> do not rely on water evaporating, so they are less bothered by humidity, but most still list a minimum around 40 to 50°F and need good ventilation and caution around ignition sources. Our <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-driveway-sealer/">oil-based vs. water-based comparison</a> covers the trade-offs in detail.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical minimum</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most sensitive to</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50°F rising</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-85°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold nights, heavy humidity</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coal tar emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50°F rising</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-85°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold nights; check local bans</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic / polymer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50°F rising</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-90°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very hot pavement</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Around 40-50°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-85°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heat, fumes, ignition sources</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Crack filler has its own rules. Many pourable asphalt crack fillers list a 50°F minimum as well, and hot-applied rope fillers depend on the pavement being dry and not frozen. Fill cracks a day or two before sealing so the filler is not still soft when the topcoat goes on. Our guide to the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> lists the application temperatures for each pick.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calendar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Turning the Numbers Into a Date on the Calendar
          </h2>

          <p>
            In most of the northern half of the US, the reliable window runs from late May through early September. Spring days can hit 70°F while nights still dip into the 40s, which fails the overnight test. Early fall often has ideal afternoons and nights that cool off fast under clear skies. If you must seal in shoulder season, pick a stretch with cloudy nights and lows in the mid-50s.
          </p>

          <p>
            Across the Southeast and Gulf Coast, the issue flips: summer heat and afternoon storms make June through August harder, while April, May, late September and October are often better. In the desert Southwest, early mornings in spring and fall are the safe bet, because summer pavement temperatures can climb well past what any water-based sealer is designed for. The Pacific Northwest has a short, dependable dry window from July into early September.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the hourly forecast two or three days out and again the morning of. You want a block that covers your application time, the following 24 hours, and ideally a second dry day for traffic. If the timing still feels tight, push to the next good window. Our pages on <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">the best time of year to seal</a> and <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">how long sealer takes to dry</a> go deeper on seasonal planning and cure times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the bigger schedule in mind. A properly applied coat on a sound driveway usually lasts two to three years, so there is no reason to rush one into a marginal weekend. If you are unsure whether the drive even needs it this year, our guide to <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> lays out the signs that it is time and the signs that it can wait.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the afternoon high alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 68°F afternoon means little if the night drops to 43°F. The sealer is still curing after dark, and the label's 24-hour window exists for that reason. Read the hourly forecast through the next morning and treat any low near the label minimum as a reason to wait for a warmer stretch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the shaded strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The section along the garage or under a tree can be ten to fifteen degrees colder than the open drive. If it reads below the minimum, it will cure weak while the rest looks perfect. Take infrared readings in the coldest spot before deciding, and seal that area another day if needed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spreading on scorching pavement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pavement above roughly 130°F flashes water out of the film before it levels, leaving lap marks and a rough, streaky surface. Start early in the morning in midsummer, mist the slab lightly if the label allows it, and work small sections while the surface is still cool and damp.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting too late in the day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Beginning a full driveway at 4 p.m. in autumn gives the cure to the coldest hours and invites dew onto a soft film. Plan to finish by early afternoon in spring and fall so the coat gets the warmest part of the day to set up before evening.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Watering down sealer for easier spreading</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Adding extra water to make a thick sealer work on a hot or cold day lowers the solids that form the protective film. Only add the amount the label allows. If the product will not spread properly in the current conditions, the conditions are the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the lowest temperature you can seal a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most water-based asphalt and coal tar sealers list 50°F and rising as the minimum for both air and pavement, with temperatures staying above 50°F for the next 24 hours. A few products ask for 55°F. Treat the label number as an absolute floor and aim for 60°F or warmer during application to leave room for forecast error and shaded sections.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal my driveway at 90 degrees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but the pavement may be far hotter than the air, often 130°F or more in full sun. Seal early in the morning before the slab heats up, mist the surface lightly if your label permits it, and work in smaller sections. Many labels set about 90°F air temperature as an upper limit, so check yours before starting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does it matter if it gets cold the night after sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The film keeps curing for hours after you finish, and if the pavement drops below about 50°F during that time, the particles may not fuse properly. The coat can look fine but powder, streak or peel later. Check the hourly forecast and skip any day with a low near or below the label minimum.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I check my driveway's surface temperature?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a handheld infrared thermometer. Point it at several spots, including the sunniest section and any shaded strip, and note the lowest and highest readings. The lowest must meet the label minimum, and the highest should be below roughly 130°F. Readings change quickly as the sun moves, so check again right before you begin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is morning or afternoon better for sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the season. In spring and fall, late morning to early afternoon is best because the pavement has warmed and there are still hours of heat left for curing. In midsummer, early morning is better because the slab has not yet baked in the sun, and the coat cures through a warm afternoon.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does humidity change how long sealer needs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It does. Water-based sealers dry by evaporation, so high humidity slows the process significantly. On a humid day, expect the dry and rain-safe times on the label to stretch by half or more. Watch for dew in the evening as well, since condensation on a soft film can leave a dull or blotchy finish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal a driveway in October?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In many northern areas, October is risky because nights often drop into the 40s even after warm afternoons. If you get a stretch with cloudy nights and lows in the mid-50s, it can work. In the South and Southwest, October is often one of the better months. Let the forecast decide rather than the calendar.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

