        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Seal when the air is in the 60s to mid-80s, the coldest patch of pavement reads at least 50°F, and the next 24 hours stay above that line. Check all three readings, not just the afternoon high, and give yourself a cushion of five to ten degrees over the label minimum.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the forecast forces a choice between sealing on a marginal day and waiting a week, wait. A coat applied in good conditions lasts years. One applied in the cold usually has to be scrubbed off and redone, which costs twice the sealer and twice the weekend.
          </p>
